1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 d5 3. c4 e6 4. Nc3 Bb4 5. Bg5 Nbd7 6. e3 c5 7. cxd5 exd5 8. Bd3 c4 9. Bf5 Nb6 10. Bxc8 Rxc8 11. O-O O-O 12. Ne5 ({A good illustration of Black's ideas and intentions in this position is the following game:} 12. Qc2 Be7 13. Nd2 Ng4 14. Bxe7 Qxe7 15. Rfe1 Rc6 16. Nf1 f5 17. f3 Nf6 18. Rad1 Qd7 19. Qf2 Na8 20. Qd2 Nc7 21. e4 fxe4 22. fxe4 Ng4 23. Re2 Rcf6 24. Ne3 Nf2 25. Rf1 Nxe4 26. Nxe4 Rxf1+ 27. Nxf1 dxe4 28. Rxe4 b5 $17 {Murugan-Zvjaginsev, Goodricke op 1997.} (28... b5 29. Ne3 Ne6 30. d5 Nc5 31. Rd4 Qd6 32. Rg4 Nd3 33. Qc3 Qh6 {0-1 Murugan,K-Zvjaginsev,V/Goodricke op 1997/EXP 58} (33... Qh6 34. Qd4 {0-1 Murugan,K-Zvjaginsev,V/Goodricke 1997/EXP 56}))) 12... Be7 13. Bxf6 Bxf6 14. a4 a5 15. f4 {Since Black cannot utilize the weakness of the e4-square, this serves White well. However, Black's position is solid enough, as the game proves.} Qe7 16. Qf3 Rcd8 17. Rf2 Bxe5 18. dxe5 f6 $1 19. exf6 Qxf6 20. Rd1 Qc6 21. Rfd2 Nxa4 22. Nxa4 Qxa4 23. Rxd5 Rxd5 24. Qxd5+ Kh8 25. Kf2 h6 26. Rd2 Qb4 27. Ke2 c3 28. bxc3 Qxc3 29. Qxb7 Re8 30. Qb6 a4 31. Qd4 Qxd4 32. Rxd4 Ra8 33. Rd2 a3 34. Ra2 Kg8 35. Kd2 Kf7 36. Kc2 Ke6 37. Kb1 Kf5 38. Rc2 Ke4 39. Rc7 Kxe3 40. Rxg7 Kxf4 41. Rg6 h5 42. h4 a2+ 43. Ka1 Ra3 44. Rg5 Rg3 1/2-1/2

